        <abstract>
          <p>
            <bold>syn90020</bold>
          </p>
          <p><bold>Background: </bold>Brain metastases may acquire genomic features that differ from the primary tumor and extracranial disease. We compared matched specimens to identify recurrent evolutionary patterns. <bold>Methods: </bold>Targeted sequencing was performed on 52 matched primary tumors, extracranial metastases, and brain metastases. Somatic alterations, copy-number changes, and clonal relationships were evaluated. <bold>Results: </bold>Brain metastases showed enrichment of alterations affecting the PI3K pathway and cell-cycle regulation. In 17 patients, potentially actionable alterations were present in the brain specimen but absent from the primary tumor. <bold>Conclusions: </bold>Matched analysis demonstrated clinically relevant genomic divergence in a subset of brain metastases and supports tissue or liquid-biopsy reassessment when feasible. Research Sponsor: None. </p>
        </abstract>
